    'lessons' => [
        'vocabulary-a1-1' => ['skill' => 'vocabulary', 'level' => 'A1', 'title' => 'Everyday Objects and the Classroom', 'content' => "## What you'll learn\nAround the classroom there are things you use every day: a desk, a chair, a board, a book, a pen and a pencil. Learning these words helps you follow lessons and ask for things. After this lesson you can name the objects around you and say what you have.\n\n## How it works\nStart with the big things in the room. You sit on a chair and write at a desk. The teacher writes on the board, and students read the new words. Things you use for writing are a pen, a pencil, a ruler and an eraser, kept in a pencil case.\n\nSmall objects have easy names: a book is something you read, a notebook is for your own writing, and a schoolbag carries your books to school.\n\nWhen you learn a new word, say it aloud and point to the object so you remember it faster. Try one short sentence for each word, for example: This is my desk.\n\n> I have a new schoolbag with two books, a pencil case and a ruler inside.\n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"I sit on the table\" — a table is for putting things on; you sit on a chair.\n- \"I have one books\" — with one, use the singular: \"I have one book\".\n\n## Try it yourself\nLook around your room and name five objects in English. Then write two sentences with the words chair, book and pen."],
        'vocabulary-a1-2' => ['skill' => 'vocabulary', 'level' => 'A1', 'title' => 'Family Words and Numbers 1-20', 'content' => "## What you'll learn\nYour family is the first thing you talk about in a new language. You will learn words like mother, father, brother, sister and grandparents, and numbers from one to twenty. After this lesson you can say who is in your family and how old people are.\n\n## How it works\nYour mother and father are your parents. A brother is a boy or man with the same parents as you, and a sister is a girl or woman. Your parents' parents are your grandparents, and the children of your aunt or uncle are your cousins.\n\nFor numbers, learn them in groups. One, two, three, four, five, six, seven, eight, nine and ten are the first ten. Then eleven, twelve, thirteen, fourteen, fifteen, sixteen, seventeen, eighteen, nineteen, twenty. Notice that thirteen to nineteen end in \"-teen\".\n\nPut the two sets together: \"I have two brothers\", \"My sister is nine\", \"My grandmother is sixty\". Use is for one person and are for two or more: My father is forty, my parents are happy.\n\n> My family is small: my mother, my father, my brother and me. My brother is sixteen.\n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"My brother is sixteens\" — numbers never take -s: \"My brother is sixteen\".\n- \"I have two brother\" — after two, use the plural: \"I have two brothers\".\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite four sentences about your family with a number in each one: ages, or how many brothers and sisters you have."],
        'vocabulary-a1-3' => ['skill' => 'vocabulary', 'level' => 'A1', 'title' => 'Food, Drinks and Basic Colours', 'content' => "## What you'll learn\nFood and drink words help you shop, order in a café and talk about meals. In this lesson you learn common foods like bread, apple and rice, drinks like tea and juice, and the basic colours. After the lesson you can say what you like and what colour things are.\n\n## How it works\nSome foods are fruits, like apple, banana and orange. Some are vegetables, like onion, carrot and potato. Other everyday foods are bread, rice, eggs, milk, cheese and chicken. Drinks are water, tea, coffee, juice and milk. A banana is yellow, an apple can be red or green, and an orange is orange!\n\nThe basic colours are red, blue, green, yellow, black, white, brown, pink, purple and grey. Use them like this: a black pen, a white cup, a green apple. The colour comes before the noun.\n\nTo talk about meals, say \"I have breakfast in the morning\", \"I have lunch at twelve\" and \"I have dinner in the evening\". To say what you like, use \"I like tea\" or \"I don't like coffee\".\n\n> I have breakfast every morning: bread with cheese, and a cup of tea.\n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"I like coffee very\" — put very before the adjective: \"I like coffee very much\".\n- \"She like apples\" — with he or she add -s: \"She likes apples\".\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite three sentences about what you eat and drink today, and say the colour of one food in each sentence."],
        'vocabulary-a2-1' => ['skill' => 'vocabulary', 'level' => 'A2', 'title' => 'Travel Words: Getting Around the City', 'content' => "## What you'll learn\nWhen you travel, you need words for places, transport and directions. This lesson gives you the key words: airport, station, ticket, platform, flight and tourist. After the lesson you can ask for a ticket, find your platform and describe a journey.\n\n## How it works\nPlaces: you catch a plane at the airport, and a train at the station. In the station, you wait on the platform and buy a ticket at the office. A journey by plane is a flight; by train, a journey or a ride.\n\nTransport words: a bus, a tram, a taxi and the underground (the metro). You catch a bus at a bus stop. As a tourist in another city, you stay in a hotel.\n\nUseful phrases: \"A ticket to London, please\", \"Which platform for the train to Oxford?\", \"Where is the bus stop?\" To ask the way, say \"Excuse me, how do I get to the centre?\" Remember: \"go by bus\", \"go on foot\", not \"go with bus\".\n\n> Excuse me, two tickets to the airport, please. Which platform does the bus leave from?\n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"I go to school with bus\" — use by: \"I go to school by bus\".\n- \"I arrived to the station\" — you arrive in or at a place, never \"arrive to\": \"I arrived at the station\".\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite three sentences about how you travel around your city, and where you buy a ticket."],
        'vocabulary-a2-2' => ['skill' => 'vocabulary', 'level' => 'A2', 'title' => 'Shopping for Clothes and Essentials', 'content' => "## What you'll learn\nShopping words help you buy clothes, food and everyday things with confidence. You will learn the names of shops, clothes and useful phrases like \"How much is it?\" After the lesson you can shop in a supermarket or a clothes shop and understand prices.\n\n## How it works\nShops: you buy food at a supermarket or a bakery (bread and cakes), and medicine at a pharmacy. A butcher's sells meat, and a florist sells flowers. You can also shop in a market, where prices are often lower.\n\nClothes: you wear a T-shirt, a shirt, trousers, a dress, a jacket and shoes. On your feet you wear socks, and in winter a scarf and gloves keep you warm. Before you buy clothes, you try them on in a changing room.\n\nUseful phrases: \"How much is this T-shirt?\", \"Do you have this in a smaller size?\", \"Can I pay by card?\" Remember: when something is cheap it costs little, and when it is expensive it costs a lot.\n\n> Excuse me, how much is this jacket? And do you have it in a smaller size?\n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"I buyed a new shirt\" — the past of buy is irregular: \"I bought a new shirt\".\n- \"How much are this shoes?\" — shoes is plural: \"How much are these shoes?\"\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite a short conversation: you ask the price of a jacket in a shop, and the assistant answers."],
        'vocabulary-a2-3' => ['skill' => 'vocabulary', 'level' => 'A2', 'title' => 'Hobbies and Words for Daily Routines', 'content' => "## What you'll learn\nTo talk about your free time and your day, you need verbs like paint, swim, cook and read, and time words like usually and sometimes. After this lesson you can describe your hobby and your daily routine clearly.\n\n## How it works\nHobbies are things you enjoy doing: painting, swimming, reading, playing football, cooking and taking photos. Say \"I enjoy painting\" or \"I like playing football\" — after like and enjoy, the verb ends in -ing.\n\nDaily routine verbs: get up, have a shower, have breakfast, go to work, have lunch, do homework, watch TV, go to bed. Use time words to make your routine clear: usually, sometimes, always, never. They go before the main verb: \"I usually get up at seven\".\n\nPut it together: \"In the morning I get up at six and have a shower. Then I have breakfast. In the evening I watch TV or read a book. I usually go to bed at ten.\"\n\n> On Saturdays I usually go swimming with my friends, and sometimes we cook dinner together.\n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"I go to swim every week\" — say \"I go swimming\" or \"I swim\".\n- \"I always am happy\" — always goes before the main verb: \"I am always happy\".\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite three sentences about your hobby, and three sentences about your daily routine with the words usually and sometimes."],
        'vocabulary-b1-1' => ['skill' => 'vocabulary', 'level' => 'B1', 'title' => 'Work and Job Hunting Vocabulary', 'content' => "## What you'll learn\nWords about work help you write a CV, talk about your job and understand job adverts. In this lesson you learn salary, employee, employer, interview and phrasal verbs like carry on. After the lesson you can talk about your working life with confidence.\n\n## How it works\nThe person who works is an employee, and the person or company that pays them is the employer. The money you receive each month is your salary. When you finish a job, you resign or hand in your notice.\n\nJob hunting: you send your CV and a cover letter to a company. If they like your application, you get an interview. In the interview, talk about your skills and experience. Someone with no job is unemployed.\n\nCommon verbs: apply for a job, get a job, start work, carry on (continue) and give up (stop). Job adverts often say \"full-time position\" and \"good salary\".\n\n> I am applying for a job as a sales assistant, and I have an interview next Monday at ten.\n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"I work for 5 years\" — with a period up to now, use the present perfect: \"I have worked here for 5 years\".\n- \"She is a doctor in a hospital\" — say \"She is a doctor at a hospital\".\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite four sentences about your job, or the job you want: the salary, the hours, and what you do."],
        'vocabulary-b1-2' => ['skill' => 'vocabulary', 'level' => 'B1', 'title' => 'Health, Fitness and Healthy Habits', 'content' => "## What you'll learn\nHealth words help you talk to a doctor, describe how you feel and give advice about healthy living. You will learn words like fit, ill and exhausted, and phrasal verbs like get over. After the lesson you can describe symptoms and give simple advice.\n\n## How it works\nHow you feel: fit means healthy and strong, ill means sick, and exhausted means very tired. If you have a cold, a headache or a temperature, you should see a doctor. To recover from an illness, you get over it.\n\nHealthy habits: take regular exercise, eat a balanced diet with fruit and vegetables, drink enough water and sleep well. Unhealthy habits include smoking, eating too much sugar and sleeping very little.\n\nGiving advice: use should and shouldn't. \"You should rest\", \"You shouldn't work too hard\". To talk about staying well, say \"keep fit\" and \"stay healthy\".\n\n> I felt exhausted all week, but I got over my cold quickly by resting and drinking lots of water.\n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"I am feeling tired since morning\" — since needs the present perfect: \"I have felt tired since morning\".\n- \"You should to rest\" — after should use the base verb: \"You should rest\".\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite two pieces of advice for a friend who feels ill, using should and shouldn't."],
        'vocabulary-b1-3' => ['skill' => 'vocabulary', 'level' => 'B1', 'title' => 'Common Phrasal Verbs in Everyday English', 'content' => "## What you'll learn\nPhrasal verbs are very common in spoken English, so understanding them helps you sound more natural. In this lesson you learn take up, put off, give up, run into and bring up. After the lesson you can use these verbs in everyday conversations.\n\n## How it works\nA phrasal verb is a verb plus a small word, and together they have a new meaning. Take up means to start a new hobby: \"I took up painting last year\". Put off means to postpone: \"Let's put off the meeting until Friday\". Give up means to stop trying: \"Don't give up!\"\n\nRun into means to meet someone by chance: \"I ran into an old friend\". Bring up means to raise a child or mention a topic: \"She brought up three children\". Get over means to recover: \"I got over my cold\".\n\nThe particle changes the meaning, so learn each verb as one unit. With a pronoun like it, put it in the middle: \"put it off\", not \"put off it\".\n\n> I ran into my old teacher yesterday; she took up tennis after she retired.\n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"I will give up it\" — pronouns go in the middle: \"I will give it up\".\n- \"She put off the meeting for Friday\" — say \"put off the meeting until Friday\".\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite four sentences, each with one phrasal verb from this lesson: take up, put off, give up, run into."],
        'vocabulary-b2-1' => ['skill' => 'vocabulary', 'level' => 'B2', 'title' => 'Phrasal Verbs for Business Meetings', 'content' => "## What you'll learn\nIn meetings, native speakers use phrasal verbs all the time: put off, carry out, look into, go ahead, set up. This lesson teaches the most useful ones. After the lesson you can talk about projects and decisions naturally.\n\n## How it works\ncarry out means to do or implement: \"We carried out the plan on time\". Look into means to investigate: \"We are looking into the problem\". Go ahead means to proceed: \"The project is going well, so we decided to go ahead\".\n\nPut off means to postpone: \"We put the meeting off until Thursday\". Set up means to start: \"She set up her own company in 2019\". Draw up means to prepare a document: \"Let's draw up a contract\". Bring up means to mention a topic.\n\nWith business phrasal verbs, the pronoun goes in the middle: \"We put it off\". Separable verbs can also take the noun at the end: \"carry out the plan\" or \"carry the plan out\".\n\n> We had to put off the launch, but after looking into the delays, we decided to go ahead.\n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"We carried on the plan\" — carry on means continue; the verb is carry out.\n- \"Let's look it into\" — the particle stays at the end: \"Let's look into it\".\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite four sentences using carry out, look into, go ahead and set up about a real or imaginary project."],
        'vocabulary-b2-2' => ['skill' => 'vocabulary', 'level' => 'B2', 'title' => 'Formal and Informal Register in English', 'content' => "## What you'll learn\nEnglish changes with the situation: you write an email to a company differently from a text to a friend. In this lesson you learn the difference between formal and informal words and phrases. After the lesson you can choose the right register for your message.\n\n## How it works\nFormal English is used in letters, reports and emails to people you do not know. It uses longer words: obtain instead of get, purchase instead of buy, assist instead of help, request instead of ask for. Informal English is for friends and family: kids, stuff, loads of.\n\nSet phrases matter too. Formally you write \"I am writing to enquire about...\" and close with \"Yours faithfully\" or \"Yours sincerely\". Informally you write \"Hi!\" and close with \"Cheers!\" or \"See you!\".\n\nThe verb is often the same; the register changes the words around it. \"Please find attached\" is formal; \"Here's the file\" is informal. Practise translating one sentence into both registers so the difference becomes automatic.\n\n> Please find attached my application form. I look forward to your prompt response.\n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- Mixing registers: \"Dear Sir or Madam, ... Cheers!\" — the closing must match the opening: use \"Yours faithfully,\".\n- \"I want to obtain some info\" — mixed: say \"I would like to obtain some information\" or keep the whole message informal.\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite the same message twice: once to a manager (formal) and once to a friend (informal)."],
        'vocabulary-b2-3' => ['skill' => 'vocabulary', 'level' => 'B2', 'title' => 'Abstract Nouns and Useful Collocations', 'content' => "## What you'll learn\nAbstract nouns name ideas you cannot touch: freedom, success, responsibility, empathy. Collocations are words that naturally go together, like make a decision or heavy rain. After this lesson you can choose the right noun and the right partner word.\n\n## How it works\nAbstract nouns: freedom, justice, knowledge, success, failure, responsibility, empathy (understanding another person's feelings). You cannot see or touch them, but they are central to discussion and opinion writing.\n\nA collocation is the natural combination of words. In English we make a decision, take a photo, do homework, pay attention, heavy rain, strong coffee, fast food. We do not say \"do a decision\" or \"big rain\" — it sounds wrong to native speakers.\n\nThe best way to learn collocations is in groups with the verb: make a decision, make a mistake, make an effort, make progress. Then with the noun: take responsibility, take a break, take part. Record them with an example sentence, and use them in your writing.\n\n> She finally made a decision about her career, and she takes responsibility for the result.\n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"He did a mistake\" — the verb is make: \"He made a mistake\".\n- \"It was a big rain\" — the collocation is heavy: \"It was heavy rain\".\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite five collocations with make or take, and use each one in a short sentence."],
        'vocabulary-c1-1' => ['skill' => 'vocabulary', 'level' => 'C1', 'title' => 'Academic Vocabulary for Building Arguments', 'content' => "## What you'll learn\nAcademic writing needs precise words: analysis, evaluation, hypothesis, objective, paradox. These nouns and adjectives let you build and judge arguments accurately. After this lesson you can discuss ideas in a clear, academic way.\n\n## How it works\nAnalysis means examining the parts of something to understand the whole. Evaluation is a careful judgement of value, weighing strengths and weaknesses. A hypothesis is an idea you test with evidence, and a paradox is a statement that seems to contradict itself but may be true.\n\nAdjectives matter: objective means based on facts, subjective means based on personal feelings. \"To shed light on\" an issue means to make it clearer. A coherent argument is logical and well organised.\n\nUse these words to frame your writing: \"This analysis will evaluate...\", \"The evidence supports the hypothesis\", \"It is important to remain objective\". Academic English prefers nouns and precision: evaluation rather than \"looking at things\", and evidence rather than \"proof in general\".\n\n> A thorough analysis of the data is needed before we can make an objective evaluation of the proposal.\n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"We need more proofs\" — evidence is uncountable: \"We need more evidence\".\n- \"It is very important\" — vague in academic writing; choose a precise frame: \"This paper argues that...\".\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite two sentences that use analysis and evaluation correctly in an academic context."],
        'vocabulary-c1-2' => ['skill' => 'vocabulary', 'level' => 'C1', 'title' => 'Precise Collocations for Formal Writing', 'content' => "## What you'll learn\nFormal writing depends on exact collocations: pose a question, draw a conclusion, cite evidence, make a difference. Using the right partner word shows precision and fluency. After this lesson you can write formal sentences that sound natural and exact.\n\n## How it works\nSome collocations are fixed. You pose a question (not \"make a question\"), draw a conclusion (not \"create a conclusion\"), and cite evidence (not \"mention evidence\"). You also reach a conclusion, raise an issue, and address a problem.\n\nAdverbs collocate too: utterly convinced, deeply concerned, highly unlikely, strongly suggest. Choose them carefully: \"The findings strongly suggest...\" is academic, while \"really show\" is too informal.\n\nThe difference between near-synonyms matters. \"Make a difference\" means to have a positive effect; \"do a favour\" is about helping one person. \"Heavy workload\" and \"heavy rain\" share heavy, but \"big workload\" would be wrong. Learn each phrase as a whole, with its exact partner.\n\n> The report poses a difficult question, and the authors draw the conclusion that urgent action is required.\n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"I want to make a question\" — the verb is pose or raise: \"I would like to pose a question\".\n- \"The results really show...\" — too informal; use \"The results strongly suggest...\".\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite three formal sentences using pose a question, draw a conclusion and cite evidence."],
        'vocabulary-c1-3' => ['skill' => 'vocabulary', 'level' => 'C1', 'title' => 'Nuance and Discourse Markers in Context', 'content' => "## What you'll learn\nDiscourse markers organise your writing: furthermore, nevertheless, consequently, admittedly. Hedging words like to a certain extent add nuance. After this lesson you can connect ideas precisely and express claims with the right degree of certainty.\n\n## How it works\nDiscourse markers guide the reader. Furthermore and moreover add a new point; nevertheless and however introduce contrast; consequently and hence show results; admittedly concedes a point before you argue against it.\n\nNuance means expressing exactly how true or important something is. Instead of \"X is bad\", say \"X may be problematic to a certain extent\". Use \"it could be argued that\", \"tends to\", \"appears to\" and \"arguably\" to soften strong claims.\n\nPosition matters. At the start of a sentence, a marker is followed by a comma: \"Nevertheless, the plan succeeded.\" In the middle, it is set off by commas: \"The plan, nevertheless, succeeded.\" Overuse weakens your writing, so choose the marker that fits the logical relationship exactly.\n\n> The proposal is costly; nevertheless, it could be argued that the long-term benefits justify the investment.\n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"Actually\" misused as \"currently\": \"Actually, the results show...\" — actually means \"in fact\"; for now use \"currently\".\n- \"However, furthermore\" together — pick one marker; stacking them is redundant.\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite a short paragraph of four sentences using furthermore, nevertheless and consequently correctly."],
        'writing-a1-1' => ['skill' => 'writing', 'level' => 'A1', 'title' => 'Writing: Introduce Yourself in Sentences', 'content' => "## What you'll learn\nIntroducing yourself is the first writing you do in a new language. You learn to say your name, age and where you live in simple sentences. After this lesson you can write a short introduction about yourself.\n\n## How it works\nA simple introduction has three sentences. First your name: \"My name is Sara.\" or \"I am Sara.\" Then your age: \"I am twenty years old.\" Then where you live: \"I live in Rabat.\" Put the sentences in order and you have a short introduction.\n\nUse the verb to be: I am, you are, he is, she is, it is, we are, they are. For age, we say \"I am twenty\", not \"I have twenty\". With live, we say \"I live in a city\" or \"I live in a small town\".\n\nKeep sentences short and finish each one with a full stop. Start with a capital letter for \"I\" and for names. Before you write, say the sentences aloud; what you say easily is usually what you can write.\n\n> My name is Omar. I am twenty-five years old and I live in Casablanca.\n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"I have twenty years\" — say \"I am twenty years old\".\n- \"i live in rabat\" — begin with a capital letter and end with a full stop: \"I live in Rabat.\"\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite three sentences: your name, your age, and the place where you live."],
        'writing-a1-2' => ['skill' => 'writing', 'level' => 'A1', 'title' => 'Writing: Sentences about My Family', 'content' => "## What you'll learn\nTalking about family is a natural part of learning English. You will write simple sentences about the people in your family and what they do. After this lesson you can write a short text about your family.\n\n## How it works\nStart with the people: \"My family is small. There are four people in my family.\" Then name each person: \"This is my mother, my father, my brother and me.\" Add one fact about each person: \"My mother is a teacher. My father is a doctor. My brother is nine.\"\n\nUse the verb to be for describing people: \"She is kind\", \"They are happy\". For things people have, use have and has: \"I have two brothers\", \"My sister has a cat\". Remember: he, she and it take has; I, you, we and they take have.\n\nUse and to join two ideas: \"My mother is a teacher and my father is a doctor.\" Say your family sentences aloud first, and then write the words you know.\n\n> My family is small. My mother is a teacher and my father is a doctor. I have one sister; she is six.\n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"My mother is teacher\" — we say \"a teacher\": \"My mother is a teacher\".\n- \"She have one sister\" — with she, use has: \"She has one sister\".\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite four sentences about your family: who is in it, and one fact about each person."],
        'writing-a1-3' => ['skill' => 'writing', 'level' => 'A1', 'title' => 'Writing: My Day in Simple Sentences', 'content' => "## What you'll learn\nWriting about your day uses very common verbs: get up, have, go, watch. In this lesson you put them into short sentences about the morning, afternoon and evening. After this lesson you can write a simple description of your day.\n\n## How it works\nFirst the morning: \"I get up at seven o'clock. I have breakfast at seven thirty. I go to school by bus.\" At noon: \"I have lunch at one o'clock. I go home at four.\" In the evening: \"I do my homework. I watch TV. I go to bed at ten.\"\n\nUse time words to show the order: in the morning, at noon, in the evening, at night. With times we say at: \"at seven\", \"at one o'clock\".\n\nUse I and the base verb: \"I get up\", \"I have breakfast\", \"I watch TV\". Join two with and: \"I get up at seven and I have breakfast.\" Write about your own day, so the sentences are true for you.\n\n> I get up at seven o'clock. I have breakfast, and then I go to school by bus. In the evening I watch TV.\n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"I get up at the morning\" — say \"in the morning\" but \"at seven\".\n- \"I have breakfast in 7 o'clock\" — with times use at: \"at 7 o'clock\".\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite five sentences about your day: what you do in the morning, at noon and in the evening."],
        'writing-a2-1' => ['skill' => 'writing', 'level' => 'A2', 'title' => 'Writing: Describe Your Daily Routine', 'content' => "## What you'll learn\nDescribing a routine shows you can use the present simple and time words together. This lesson helps you write a short paragraph about a normal day. After the lesson you can describe your daily routine clearly and in order.\n\n## How it works\nA routine paragraph follows the day: \"I usually get up at six thirty and have a shower. Then I have breakfast and leave home at eight. I have lunch at work, and I go home at six. In the evening I cook dinner and watch a film. I usually go to bed at eleven.\"\n\nUse adverbs of frequency before the main verb: \"I usually get up\", \"I sometimes have lunch at home\", \"I never miss breakfast\". Use first, then and after that to order your actions.\n\nConnect your sentences with and, but and so: \"I am tired, so I go to bed early.\" Remember: this paragraph is about every day, so use the present simple.\n\n> I usually get up at seven, and after that I go for a run. Then I have breakfast and start work at nine.\n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"Every day I gets up at six\" — with I, the verb has no -s: \"I get up\".\n- \"I make my homework\" — the verb is do: \"I do my homework\".\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite six sentences about your routine, from getting up to going to bed, with usually and after that."],
        'writing-a2-2' => ['skill' => 'writing', 'level' => 'A2', 'title' => 'Writing: A Postcard from a Holiday', 'content' => "## What you'll learn\nA postcard is a short, friendly message from a holiday. You use the past simple to say what you did and the present to say what you are doing now. After this lesson you can write a simple postcard to a friend.\n\n## How it works\nA postcard has three parts: a greeting, the news, and a closing question. Start with \"Dear Sam,\" and say where you are: \"We are in Agadir. The weather is beautiful.\" Then the news in the past simple: \"Yesterday we went to the beach. We visited the old town and ate fresh fish.\"\n\nThe past simple of go is went, of eat is ate, of stay is stayed. For negatives use didn't with the base verb: \"It didn't rain at all.\" For the present, use am, is and are: \"We are staying in a small hotel.\"\n\nEnd with a question to your friend: \"How are you? See you soon!\" Keep the tone friendly and light, like a short message.\n\n> We are having a great time in Agadir! Yesterday we went to the beach and ate fresh fish for lunch.\n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"We goed to the beach\" — the past of go is went.\n- \"It didn't rained\" — after didn't use the base verb: \"It didn't rain\".\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite a postcard of five sentences: where you are, what the weather is like, and what you did yesterday."],
        'writing-a2-3' => ['skill' => 'writing', 'level' => 'A2', 'title' => 'Writing: Paragraphs about My Hobby', 'content' => "## What you'll learn\nWriting about a hobby lets you practise like and enjoy with -ing, and the connector because. You learn to say what you do, when you do it, and why you like it. After this lesson you can write a short paragraph about your hobby.\n\n## How it works\nA hobby paragraph answers three questions. What: \"I like playing football.\" When: \"I play at the weekend.\" Why: \"I enjoy it because it is exciting and it keeps me fit.\"\n\nAfter like, enjoy and love, the verb takes -ing: \"I enjoy painting\", \"I love reading\". Use because to explain the reason: \"I like swimming because it is relaxing.\" Use it to talk about your hobby without repeating the name: \"I started it when I was ten.\"\n\nOrder the sentences: the hobby, when you do it, then the reason. \"My favourite hobby is photography. I usually take photos on Sundays. I enjoy it because it helps me see the world differently.\"\n\n> My favourite hobby is swimming. I usually swim at the weekend, and I enjoy it because it relaxes me.\n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"I enjoy to play football\" — after enjoy use -ing: \"I enjoy playing football\".\n- \"I like it because it is relax\" — the adjective is relaxing: \"it is relaxing\".\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite a paragraph of four sentences about a hobby: what it is, when you do it, and why you like it."],
        'writing-b1-1' => ['skill' => 'writing', 'level' => 'B1', 'title' => 'Writing: Give Your Opinion Clearly', 'content' => "## What you'll learn\nGiving an opinion in writing means stating your view and supporting it with a reason. This lesson teaches phrases like in my opinion, I think, and linking words like however. After the lesson you can write a short opinion paragraph.\n\n## How it works\nStart with your opinion: \"In my opinion, learning English online is very convenient.\" Then give a reason: \"You can study at home at any time.\" Then add another point with a linking word: \"However, you need to be self-disciplined to stay motivated.\"\n\nUseful phrases: In my opinion, I think that, I believe, It seems to me. Use however and but for contrast, because for reasons, and for example to add examples.\n\nA short opinion text has three parts: opinion, reason, and contrast or example. Keep one idea per sentence, and put a comma after phrases like \"In my opinion\" and \"However\" at the start of a sentence.\n\n> In my opinion, studying online saves time. However, it can be difficult to stay motivated at home.\n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"At my opinion\" — the phrase is \"in my opinion\".\n- \"However some people disagree\" — put a comma after the linking word: \"However, some people disagree\".\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite four sentences giving your opinion about a topic you know well, with in my opinion and however."],
        'writing-b1-2' => ['skill' => 'writing', 'level' => 'B1', 'title' => 'Writing: A Polite Cover Email', 'content' => "## What you'll learn\nA cover email introduces you to an employer and asks for a job or an interview. This lesson teaches polite phrases: Dear Sir or Madam, I would like to apply, Yours faithfully. After the lesson you can write a simple cover email.\n\n## How it works\nStart with the greeting and the purpose: \"Dear Sir or Madam, I am writing to apply for the position of sales assistant advertised on your website.\" Then introduce yourself: \"I am 24 years old and I have two years of experience in retail.\"\n\nGive one or two reasons you are suitable: \"I am hard-working and I enjoy working with people.\" Then ask for the next step politely: \"I would be happy to attend an interview at your convenience.\" Attach your CV: \"I have attached my CV to this email.\"\n\nClose formally. If you began with a name (\"Dear Mr Smith\"), end \"Yours sincerely,\". If you began with \"Dear Sir or Madam\", end \"Yours faithfully,\".\n\n> Dear Sir or Madam, I am writing to apply for the position of sales assistant. I have attached my CV to this email.\n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"I am interesting in the job\" — say \"I am interested in the job\".\n- \"Yours sincerely\" after \"Dear Sir or Madam\" — use \"Yours faithfully\" when you do not know the name.\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite a cover email of six sentences for a job you would like: greeting, purpose, experience, and a polite closing."],
        'writing-b1-3' => ['skill' => 'writing', 'level' => 'B1', 'title' => 'Writing: A Story about Last Week', 'content' => "## What you'll learn\nStories use the past simple and past continuous to say what happened and what was happening around it. This lesson helps you write a short story about last week. After the lesson you can tell a simple past story clearly.\n\n## How it works\nStart with when: \"Last week I visited my grandmother in Marrakech.\" Then give the background with the past continuous: \"While I was cooking, I burnt my hand.\" The past continuous sets the scene; the past simple gives the main events.\n\nPast simple verbs: visited, went, had, was, were, bought, saw. Use time words to show order: first, then, after that, finally. \"First we arrived at the station. Then we took a taxi to her house. Finally we had a big dinner.\"\n\nKeep the story short: a start, one or two events, and an ending. \"When I arrived, she was sleeping, so I waited. When she woke up, we talked for hours. We had a great time.\"\n\n> Last week I went to the beach with my cousins. While we were swimming, the weather changed and it started to rain.\n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"I was sleep when...\" — use the continuous: \"I was sleeping\".\n- \"While I cooked, I burnt my hand\" — for a long background action use the past continuous: \"While I was cooking...\".\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite a five-sentence story about last weekend, using one while sentence and the words first and finally."],
        'writing-b2-1' => ['skill' => 'writing', 'level' => 'B2', 'title' => 'Writing: Argue Both Sides of a Topic', 'content' => "## What you'll learn\nA balanced argument presents both sides before you give your view. This lesson teaches phrases like on the one hand, it could be argued, and consequently. After the lesson you can write a short essay that argues both sides fairly.\n\n## How it works\nIntroduce the topic and the debate: \"Working from home has become common, and opinions about it differ.\" Present one side: \"On the one hand, it saves time and money on travel.\" Present the other side: \"On the other hand, it can reduce teamwork and make people feel isolated.\"\n\nHedge your claims so they are not too strong: \"It could be argued that...\", \"This may lead to...\", \"Many people tend to...\". Conclude with a balanced view: \"While remote work saves time, it can reduce teamwork.\"\n\nUse linkers: on the one hand / on the other hand, however, moreover, consequently. A good essay shows both sides honestly, then takes a position.\n\n> On the one hand, remote work saves commuting time. On the other hand, it could be argued that teamwork suffers.\n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"In my opinion it is definitely the best\" — too strong for a balanced essay; hedge: \"It could be argued that...\".\n- \"On the other side\" — the fixed phrase is \"on the other hand\".\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite a short paragraph presenting both sides of one topic, using on the one hand and it could be argued."],
        'writing-b2-2' => ['skill' => 'writing', 'level' => 'B2', 'title' => 'Writing: Formal versus Informal Letters', 'content' => "## What you'll learn\nChoosing the right style for a letter changes your message completely. This lesson compares formal and informal letters: greetings, vocabulary and closings. After the lesson you can write a complaint letter and a friendly letter correctly.\n\n## How it works\nFormal letters: start \"Dear Mr Smith,\" or \"Dear Sir or Madam,\" and close \"Yours sincerely,\" or \"Yours faithfully,\". Use formal vocabulary: \"I am writing to complain about...\", \"I would appreciate your prompt response\", \"Please resolve this issue as soon as possible.\"\n\nInformal letters: start \"Hi Sam!\" or \"Dear Sam,\" and close \"Love,\" or \"See you soon!\". The language is relaxed: \"Thanks for your letter\", \"I can't wait to see you\".\n\nThe two registers never mix. A complaint letter must stay polite and never say \"This is rubbish\". An informal letter to a friend should not sound like a business report. Practise writing one message in both styles.\n\n> Dear Mr Smith, I am writing to complain about the delay in delivery. I would appreciate your prompt response.\n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- Mixing registers: \"Dear Mr Smith, ... Cheers!\" — the greeting and closing must match in formality.\n- \"I look forward to hear from you\" — after to in this phrase use -ing: \"I look forward to hearing from you\".\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite a short complaint about a late delivery in formal style, with a formal greeting and closing."],
        'writing-b2-3' => ['skill' => 'writing', 'level' => 'B2', 'title' => 'Writing: A Balanced Report with Hedging', 'content' => "## What you'll learn\nA report presents findings calmly and avoids overstatement. This lesson teaches hedging: tends to, appears to, suggests that, could be argued. After the lesson you can write a short, balanced report section.\n\n## How it works\nA report has a clear structure: an aim, the findings, and a conclusion. State the aim: \"This report examines the effects of flexible hours on staff productivity.\" Present the findings with hedging: \"The data suggests that productivity tends to increase in the mornings.\"\n\nHedging softens claims so they are honest: \"The findings indicate that...\", \"It appears that...\", \"This could be attributed to...\". Avoid absolute words like always, never and undoubtedly; they are rarely safe in a report.\n\nEnd with a measured conclusion: \"Overall, flexible hours appear to have a positive effect, although further research is needed.\"\n\n> The survey suggests that most employees tend to prefer starting later, although the data is not conclusive.\n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"This is undoubtedly the best solution\" — too assertive for a report; say \"This appears to be the most viable solution\".\n- \"The data suggests that prices has risen\" — data is plural: \"prices have risen\".\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite a short report section: one aim sentence, two hedged findings, and one measured conclusion."],
        'writing-c1-1' => ['skill' => 'writing', 'level' => 'C1', 'title' => 'Writing: A Structured Discussion Essay', 'content' => "## What you'll learn\nA discussion essay evaluates a topic using a clear structure: introduction, arguments, counter-arguments and conclusion. This lesson teaches discourse markers like furthermore and nevertheless, and precise hedging. After the lesson you can write a structured academic essay.\n\n## How it works\nBegin with the question and your approach: \"This essay will evaluate the role of artificial intelligence in education.\" Then argue one side with support: \"Furthermore, adaptive platforms can personalise learning for each student.\" Then the counter-argument: \"Nevertheless, concerns about data privacy remain significant.\"\n\nUse markers precisely: furthermore and moreover add; nevertheless and however contrast; consequently shows results; admittedly concedes. Hedge carefully: \"The evidence lends support to the view that...\", \"It could be argued that...\".\n\nEnd with a conclusion that weighs the arguments: \"On balance, while the benefits are considerable, the ethical questions must be addressed.\" The structure itself is half the mark — the reader should always know where they are.\n\n> Furthermore, the technology can provide instant feedback; nevertheless, its reliability in high-stakes assessment remains contested.\n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- Over-nominalisation: \"the making of a decision\" — say \"the decision-making process\" or simply \"the decision\".\n- \"Actually\" used for \"currently\": \"Actually, the technology is advancing\" — say \"Currently, the technology is advancing\".\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite an essay outline of six sentences: introduction, one further point, one contrast, and a balanced conclusion."],
        'writing-c1-2' => ['skill' => 'writing', 'level' => 'C1', 'title' => 'Writing: A Critical Book Review', 'content' => "## What you'll learn\nA critical review summarises a book and then evaluates it: its argument, evidence and style. This lesson teaches language for critique: compelling, questionable, sheds light on. After the lesson you can write a review that judges fairly, not just praises or attacks.\n\n## How it works\nOpen with the book and its aim: \"In this book, the author examines the decline of local journalism.\" Summarise briefly, then move to evaluation. Praise with precision: \"The argument is compelling and well supported by evidence.\"\n\nCriticise with nuance: \"The conclusion is somewhat questionable, as the data covers only one region.\" Use hedging so your judgement sounds fair: \"The analysis sheds light on the causes, although the solutions remain underdeveloped.\"\n\nStructure: summary, strengths, weaknesses, overall judgement. End with a verdict: \"On the whole, this is a thought-provoking study that rewards careful reading, despite its narrow focus.\"\n\n> The book is a compelling account of urban life, although its treatment of rural communities is somewhat cursory.\n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"The book is very good\" — vague; be precise: \"The argument is compelling and original\".\n- Over-nominalisation: \"the providing of evidence\" — say \"the provision of evidence\" or \"the evidence provided\".\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite a mini review of a book or film: one summary sentence, one strength, one weakness, and a final verdict."],
        'writing-c1-3' => ['skill' => 'writing', 'level' => 'C1', 'title' => 'Writing: A Persuasive Project Proposal', 'content' => "## What you'll learn\nA project proposal persuades a reader to fund or approve an idea, using evidence and confident but hedged language. This lesson teaches structure and stance: the problem, the solution, the benefits, the call to action. After the lesson you can write a persuasive proposal section.\n\n## How it works\nOpen with the problem and its importance: \"The current onboarding process costs the company significant time, and employee satisfaction is declining.\" Then propose the solution: \"This project would introduce a structured mentoring programme.\"\n\nJustify with evidence and hedging: \"Research suggests that structured onboarding increases retention by up to 20%.\" Address objections before they arise: \"Although the initial cost is not negligible, it is likely to be recovered within a year.\"\n\nEnd with a clear call to action: \"We therefore recommend approving the project for the next quarter.\" The tone is confident but measured: argue strongly, but never overstate what you cannot prove.\n\n> Research suggests that a structured mentoring scheme could reduce staff turnover, although the initial investment is not negligible.\n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"We must definitely implement this\" — persuasive but unsupported: say \"The evidence suggests that implementation would be beneficial\".\n- False friend: \"eventually\" used as \"possibly\" — \"eventually\" means \"in the end\": \"the plan may eventually be approved\".\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite a short proposal: the problem, your solution, one piece of evidence, and a call to action."],
    ],

